9.  Alicia 

Program Name:  Alicia.java 
Input File:  alicia.dat 

Alicia is currently learning about the formal definitions of Graphs and Trees. While she is easily able to visually classify a set of 
vertices and edges as either a graph or a tree, Alicia is struggling in being able to formally prove which classification it is. Here 
is what Alicia has learned so far: 
● 
A graph 𝐺= (𝑉, 𝐸) is formally defined to be composed of a set of vertices 𝑉= {𝑣1,𝑣2,…, 𝑣𝑛} and edges  
𝐸= {𝑒1,𝑒2,… , 𝑒𝑚} where edge 𝑒𝑖= (𝑢, 𝑣), 𝑢,𝑣∈𝑉 indicates that a relationship exists between vertex 𝑢 and vertex 𝑣.  
o 
A graph 𝐺 is said to be “undirected” if for all 𝑒𝑖= (𝑢, 𝑣) ∈𝐸, then 𝑒𝑗= (𝑣, 𝑢) ∈𝐸 (i.e., if a relationship exists between 
vertex 𝑢 and vertex 𝑣, then a relationship also exists between vertex 𝑣 and vertex 𝑢).  
▪ 
A graph 𝐺 is said to be “directed” if it is not undirected. 
o 
A graph 𝐺 is said to be “cyclic” if there exists some subset of edges {𝑒1,𝑒2, … , 𝑒𝑚′} = {(𝑣𝑖,𝑣𝑗),(𝑣𝑗,𝑣𝑘),… , (𝑣𝑙,𝑣𝑖)} ⊆
𝐸. (i.e., there exists some path of the form 𝑣𝑖→𝑣𝑗→𝑣𝑘⇝𝑣𝑖). Note that at least three vertices must be included in 
such a path for it to be considered cyclic. 
▪ 
A graph 𝐺 is said to be “acyclic” if it is not cyclic. 
